;
RUNNING THE BULLETIN BOARD

A - Running RBBS
B - Getting in as sysop
C - Looking after the forums and accounts

:A
RUNNING RBBS

The standard RBBS 4.1 Edit 02 board contains PERSONAL, GENERAL, CPM and
TEST areas when initialized, with one SYSOP seed account. New callers can
register and post without prior validation.

:B
GETTING IN AS SYSOP

Work at the private local console, not with the caller looking over your
shoulder. Enter RBBS, give the name SYSOP and use the generated six-character
password recorded in private SYSOP.DOC. Passwords are case-sensitive. The
displayed 123456 is a character counter, not a password.

Normal menus accept a single key. Text, names and message numbers still
require RETURN. Expert mode retains line commands and semicolon sequences such
as R;1 and R;1+. End a message with a period on its own line, then S to save.

    F   Select forum
    R   Read messages
    S   Summarize
    N   Read new messages
    K   Delete an authorized message
    C   Return to CP/M
    G   Disconnect

:C
LOOKING AFTER THE FORUMS AND ACCOUNTS

From private CP/M run:

    SYSUTLS:RBUTIL41

The numbered maintenance menu offers 4 to select a forum, 5 to add a forum, 6
to manage users, and 9 to quit. Option 3 resets the account database. It is
not an ordinary account-maintenance command. Back up before making significant
changes.

Forum names have at most eight characters, and this released board supports
eight areas total, including PERSONAL. Later RBBS revisions may differ; use
their own documentation. A few active forums are much more inviting than a
long list of empty ones. Choose subjects your callers are likely to care
about, and put a welcoming message in each new forum.

Check new accounts and look in on the discussions. Answer questions when you
can. Few things make a board seem more deserted than a question left
unanswered for weeks.

The protected executable is A15:RBBS41.COM; accounts, callers, subjects,
messages and associated data reside in I15:. The fixed public RBBS launcher
grants only the access needed for the board. Do not make those areas public.
Private mail is private from ordinary callers, not from the sysop or anyone
with access to backups. Raw TCP is unencrypted.

Do not delete state/initialized to reset credentials. SYSOP.DOC records the
original generated password. RBBS 3.7 and 4.1 data files differ; fresh release
disks are not an in-place migration.
